  "comments": "<p>5 Céntimos coin is popularly known as \"Puya\" in Venezuela</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Each year different type.<br><br><i>Obverse</i><br>Die <strong>1</strong> - large letters in fine strokes.<br>Die <strong>2</strong> - large letters in bold strokes.<br>Die <strong>3</strong> - smaller letters with serifs, \"U\" and \"B\" in REPUBLICA almost touch each other.<br><br><i>Reverse</i><br>Die <strong>A</strong> - wider script, especially visible on final letter \"O\".<br>Die <strong>B</strong> - taller script, especially visible on letter \"O\", letters \"E\" and \"N\" almost touch each other.<br><br>Picture: © Images courtesy of Alexandr Prokofyev.<br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/6257fb48e6b1f.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/6257fb48e6b1f.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br><br><i>Combinations</i><br><strong>1</strong>+<strong>A</strong>: 1974, VDM Metals.<br><strong>2</strong>+<strong>A</strong>: 1976, VDM Metals.<br><strong>3</strong>+<strong>B</strong>: 1977, Llantrisant (see the main photos of the article).</p>",
